// The weakest-precondition observation of state: a handler of state into the
// predicate transformers `((A, S) => Type, S) => Type`, from a postcondition
// on the answer and the final state, and an initial state, to the
// precondition the program needs there (Maillard et al., "Dijkstra Monads
// for All", ICFP 2019, § 3.4, uncurried, with propositions read as types).
// A scope answers its carrier, so the program it runs ends in the
// transformers' unit at its answer, and each clause is its operation's image:
// a read hands the initial state to what runs after, and a store runs what
// follows from the stored value. The scope is checked as a model of state's
// laws, so the observation identifies what the laws identify, and is a monad
// morphism from programs over state (§ 4.4 derives it from the state algebra
// at the answer `S => Type`). The normaliser runs no scope yet (#2754), so a
// precondition is computed through the fold `tree-state-wp.gandr` writes.

// `StateLaws`.
import "../effects/state.gandr" ;

/// The increment observed: read the counter, store one more, and answer the
/// unit. The scope answers the increment's predicate transformer.
@[ checks ]
def incrObserved() -> ((Unit, Integer) => Type, Integer) => Type {
  handle st : StateLaws with sort S = Integer {
    run n <- st.get();
    st.put(n + 1);
    pure fn (q) { fn (s) { q(unit, s) } }
  } with {
    pure(w) => pure w,
    oper get(k) => pure fn (q) { fn (s) { run w <- k(s); w(q, s) } },
    oper put(s, k) => pure fn (q) { fn (_old) { run w <- k(unit); w(q, s) } }
  }
}
